ns i've been ignoring this  side of the room but i can keep going to  this  yep  hi my name is emily grady and i'm a  first year mbm student as well um and  i'm curious about so a lot of the  companies i think um have had some sort  of  most of their initiatives have been  voluntary there have been probably some  government regulations involved  but i'm curious if you see any role for  like a government regulatory platform to  sort of lend legitimacy to some of the  branding efforts that these companies  are putting forward in the environmental  space  i i think the government  and i thought automotive industries is  one example of that i think the  government uh definitely has a role to  play i don't know if that's necessarily  on the on the branding side of things  because i think i mentioned uh  sometimes in in markets where the  government actually does play a fairly  big role that diminishes the brain's  ability to to uh benefit from from that  and kind of get additional perceptual  benefits if you will in addition to to  what the um some of the hardwired  benefits maybe  but then part of that is also how how  different industries are leveraging that  and obviously automotive industries an  example that uh  has found a way to kind of go beyond  what they had have to do and make it  something very successful  i think it it  certainly uh helps  uh  push the  thinking forward  and and i think one of the things one of  the reasons why i wanted to look at the  global companies like this is that you  can have an effect even if you don't  have a consensus globally right  if if a certain set of markets are are  requiring uh different things uh you can  imagine other markets lagging behind for  a while but not forever and so once you  see better products and more efficient  products you you kind of  want to bring them to other places as  well so i think  industries in general  should probably  spend more time  figuring out how they can push it but  but when that doesn't