d who's in the uh  where we could understand the impact uh  better because uh on the performance  side there's a whole set of metrics not  fully aligned not fully agreed on but  there are there's a number of things uh  that we're already looking at and it's  something that has uh been discussed uh  and that consumers are familiar with and  they can uh think through but part of of  of how we rate those metric those things  that we measure in the survey is a  function of what people tell us matters  to them so we'll ask them uh  in the automotive industry for example  there are all these things that the  company could be spending their time uh  doing they could uh focus on creating  better products more efficient products  they could  focus on recycling they could be thought  leaders and help educate the communities  and  and then we ask people to actually tell  us which ones of those  which which of those actually matter for  automotive company and which ones matter  for  a food company and it's not always the  same and so  we actually rate what they tell us  afterwards based on what matters to them  in that industry  but in the in terms of kind of defining  it is is the impact on on the planet  that we focus on in in this study as  opposed to kind of impact on communities  that they operate in for example  is that  so just thinking about those different  buckets that you're working with the  communities and the employees and and  others do you are you seeing a sort of  natural weighting by customers across  those different buckets that's different  than or in addition to  what you described earlier across the  countries  where people  generally will say impact on community  customers well general as part of brand  analyses will say impact on communities  usually outranks impacts on the  environment or on employees or  one of the reasons why we started with  just planet is it's not necessarily easy  for consumers to detangle all of that  so often what you experience 